  "title": "Sorting Algorithms",
  "lang": "en",
  "pages": [
    {
      "index": 1,
      "title": "The Sorting Problem",
      "size": [
        595.276,
        841.89
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "The Sorting Problem",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            57.024,
            54.308,
            199.5,
            21.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Given n comparable keys, produce a permutation in non-decreasing order.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            94.112,
            457.75,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "A comparison sort may only inspect keys through pairwise comparisons.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            121.112,
            445.25,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "A stable sort keeps equal keys in their original relative order.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            148.112,
            414.0,
            13.373
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "title": "Comparison Lower Bound",
      "size": [
        595.276,
        841.89
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Comparison Lower Bound",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            57.024,
            54.308,
            231.0,
            21.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Any comparison sort needs Ω(n log n) comparisons in the worst case.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            94.112,
            439.0,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "The decision tree has n! leaves, so its height is at least log2(n!).",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            121.112,
            439.0,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Stirling's approximation gives log2(n!) = Θ(n log n).",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            148.112,
            351.5,
            13.373
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "title": "Merge Sort",
      "size": [
        595.276,
        841.89
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Merge Sort",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            57.024,
            54.308,
            105.0,
            21.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Split the array in half, sort each half recursively, then merge.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            94.112,
            414.0,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "The recurrence T(n) = 2T(n/2) + Θ(n) solves to Θ(n log n).",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            121.112,
            389.0,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "It is stable but needs Θ(n) auxiliary space.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            148.112,
            295.25,
            13.373
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "title": "Quick Sort",
      "size": [
        595.276,
        841.89
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Quick Sort",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            57.024,
            54.308,
            105.0,
            21.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Partition around a pivot, then sort the two sides independently.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            94.112,
            414.0,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Average Θ(n log n); worst case Θ(n^2) when the pivot is always extreme.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            121.112,
            470.25,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "In place apart from the recursion stack, but not stable.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            148.112,
            364.0,
            13.373
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "title": "Heap Sort",
      "size": [
        595.276,
        841.89
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Heap Sort",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            57.024,
            54.308,
            94.5,
            21.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Build a max-heap in Θ(n), then repeatedly extract the maximum.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            94.112,
            407.75,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Θ(n log n) in the worst case and in place.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            121.112,
            282.75,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Not stable, and its cache behaviour is worse than quick sort in practice.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            148.112,
            470.25,
            13.373
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "title": "Insertion Sort",
      "size": [
        595.276,
        841.89
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Insertion Sort",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            57.024,
            54.308,
            147.0,
            21.0
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Repeatedly take the next key and shift larger keys one slot to the right.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            94.112,
            470.25,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Θ(n^2) in the worst case but Θ(n) on nearly sorted input.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            121.112,
            382.75,
            13.373
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullet",
          "text": "Stable and in place, which is why hybrid sorts use it for small runs.",
          "level": 0,
          "bbox": [
            59.024,
            148.112,
            445.25,
            13.373
          ]
        }
      ],
      "flags": {
        "text_layer": true
      }
    },
